ing the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the first equation by the second one, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =18 \\\\ R & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nSo, we can write the function $f(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nThe maximum value of the function is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3\\sqrt{2},$ and it is attained when the expression in the parentheses is equal to $2k\\pi ,$ where $k$ is an integer. \n\nTherefore, the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =2\\pi k \\\\ 5x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi k \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{20}+\\frac{2}{5}\\pi k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking $k=1,$ we find that the smallest positive value of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{20}+\\frac{2}{5}\\pi \\cdot 1=\\frac{7\\pi }{20}.\n$$" "The sum formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n-3\\operator