{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(25-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(25-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{9}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{59}{2}\\theta +30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{59}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,) \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{9}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0)+15(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{9}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{59\\pi }{8}+15(0-1)-\\frac{9}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{59\\pi }{8}-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(25-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +9{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(25-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{9}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{59}{2}\\theta +30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{59}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,) \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{9}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0)+15(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{9}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{59\\pi }{8}+15(0-1)-\\frac{9}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{59\\pi }{8}-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186632688 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-x\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(B)=2$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =2 \\\\ 12-2x & =2 \\\\ -2x & =2-12 \\\\ -2x & =-10 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-x\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\det (B)=2$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =2 \\\\ 12-2x & =2 \\\\ -2x & =2-12 \\\\ -2x & =-10 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186632694 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\theta -\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )){|}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-0)-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\theta -\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta )){|}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-0)-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186632702 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3+t & 4t \\\\ -3 & 3-t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=45,$ what is the value of $t?$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3+t & 4t \\\\ -3 & 3-t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $\\det (A)=45,$ what is the value of $t?$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186632702 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3+t & 4t \\\\ -3 & 3-t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3+t)(3-t)-4t(-3) \\\\  & =9-{t}^{2}+12t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}+12t+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=45$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =45 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+12t+9 & =45 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+12t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-12t+36 & =0 \\\\ (t-6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3+t & 4t \\\\ -3 & 3-t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3+t)(3-t)-4t(-3) \\\\  & =9-{t}^{2}+12t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}+12t+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (A)=45$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =45 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+12t+9 & =45 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+12t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-12t+36 & =0 \\\\ (t-6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186632718 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(4-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(4-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2})){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{8}\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}-2(2)+\\frac{1}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$, ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(4-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(4-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2})){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{8}\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}-2(2)+\\frac{1}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186632731 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =4((\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi +2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}16{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =4((\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi +2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186633565 :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}:f(t)=⟨1,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}⟩$ and ${C}_{2}:g(u)=⟨1,\\,u-1,\\,a+u⟩,$ where $a$ is a constant. Find the values of $a$ for which these two curves do not intersect." "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}:f(t)=⟨1,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}⟩$ and ${C}_{2}:g(u)=⟨1,\\,u-1,\\,a+u⟩,$ where $a$ is a constant. Find the values of $a$ for which these two curves do not intersect."] [:db/cas 17592186633565 :question/worked-solution "To find the points of intersection of the given curves, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =g(u) \\\\ ⟨1,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨1,\\,u-1,\\,a+u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components of the vectors in the above equation gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}-1 &=u-1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2} &=a+u\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $u={t}^{2}.$ Substituting $u={t}^{2}$ into the third equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2} & =a+u \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2} & =a+{t}^{2} \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡t & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the curves to have no intersection points, the final equation must have no solutions. This means that the right-hand side should be less than $0$ or greater than $1.$ \n\nSo, the curves do not intersect when $a\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" "To find the points of intersection of the given curves, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =g(u) \\\\ ⟨1,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨1,\\,u-1,\\,a+u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components of the vectors in the above equation gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}-1 &=u-1 \\\\ {\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2} &=a+u\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $u={t}^{2}.$ Substituting $u={t}^{2}$ into the third equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2} & =a+u \\\\ {\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2} & =a+{t}^{2} \\\\ {\\sin }^{2}⁡t & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the curves to have no intersection points, the final equation must have no solutions. This means that the right-hand side should be less than $0$ or greater than $1.$ \n\nSo, the curves do not intersect when $a\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186633595 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${d}_{32},$ we remove the third row and second column: \n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ & 1 \\\\ 2 & ∗ & 3 \\\\ ∗ & 1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${d}_{32}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{32} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}a & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}a & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3a-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate the minor of ${d}_{32},$ we remove the third row and second column: \n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ & 1 \\\\ 2 & ∗ & 3 \\\\ ∗ & 1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${d}_{32}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{32} & =\\det \\begin{bmatrix}a & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}a & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3a-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634691 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant–tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{8} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{8}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{64} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{64} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{64}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant–tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{8} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{8}^{2}} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{64} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1={\\sec }^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1 & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{64} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{64}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635503 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{cos}^{2}⁡3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{\\cos }^{2}⁡3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186635503 :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n4{cos}^{2}⁡3x=2(2{cos}^{2}⁡3x)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+1)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{cos}^{2}⁡3x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+2-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+2x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )+2\\pi -\\frac{1}{3}{\\pi }^{3}]-[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}] \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi -{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (6-{\\pi }^{2})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n4{\\cos }^{2}⁡3x=2(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡3x)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+1)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4{\\cos }^{2}⁡3x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+2-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+2x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )+2\\pi -\\frac{1}{3}{\\pi }^{3}]-[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}] \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi -{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (6-{\\pi }^{2})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635557 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({sin}^{2}⁡8x-{cos}^{2}⁡8x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({\\sin }^{2}⁡8x-{\\cos }^{2}⁡8x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186635557 :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =8x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡16x={cos}^{2}⁡8x-{sin}^{2}⁡8x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({sin}^{2}⁡8x-{cos}^{2}⁡8x)\\text{d}x & =-\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡8x-{sin}^{2}⁡8x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡16x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡16x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =8x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡16x={\\cos }^{2}⁡8x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡8x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({\\sin }^{2}⁡8x-{\\cos }^{2}⁡8x)\\text{d}x & =-\\int ({\\cos }^{2}⁡8x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡8x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡16x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡16x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636424 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2-6)-0+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1-9) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} &=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{-4}\\cdot 8=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2-6)-0+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1-9) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} &=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{-4}\\cdot 8=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636426 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & k & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{2}{5}$ and $det(A)=2k+3.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" "The entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & k & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{2}{5}$ and $\\det (A)=2k+3.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186636426 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=2k+3\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}^{t}={c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2k-0 \\\\  & =2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2k+3)}\\cdot (2k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{\\,2k+3\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{2}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2k}{\\,2k+3\\,} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 10k & =4k+6 \\\\ 6k & =6 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=2k+3\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}^{t}={c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2k-0 \\\\  & =2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2k+3)}\\cdot (2k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{\\,2k+3\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{2}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2k}{\\,2k+3\\,} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 10k & =4k+6 \\\\ 6k & =6 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636437 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta , \\\\ y & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(-4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta , \\\\ y & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(-4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636452 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636464 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} &=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=-\\frac{9}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} &=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=-\\frac{9}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\csc }^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636513 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1-0)-0+2(2-0) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} &=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32}=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2=\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1-0)-0+2(2-0) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} &=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32}=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2=\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186636539 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} &=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta ))}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}{csc}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} &=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta ))}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\csc }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}{\\csc }^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637264 :question/worked-solution "In general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ and the rays $\\theta ={\\theta }_{1}$ and $\\theta ={\\theta }_{2}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of our circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }144{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }72{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ and the rays $\\theta ={\\theta }_{1}$ and $\\theta ={\\theta }_{2}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of our circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }144{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }72{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637266 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}(-{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}(-{\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637266 :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}(-{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-(1) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}(-{\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-(1) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637278 :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)={x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡2-{2}^{x}$ is concave up." "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)={x}^{2}{\\ln }^{2}⁡2-{2}^{x}$ is concave up."] [:db/cas 17592186637278 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)={x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡2-{2}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x{ln}^{2}⁡2-{2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2-{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{ln}^{2}⁡2-{2}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡2 & >0 \\\\ {ln}^{2}⁡2(2-{2}^{x}) & >0 \\\\ 2-{2}^{x} & >0 \\\\ 2 & >{2}^{x} \\\\ 1 & >x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,1).$" "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)={x}^{2}{\\ln }^{2}⁡2-{2}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x{\\ln }^{2}⁡2-{2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{\\ln }^{2}⁡2-{2}^{x}{\\ln }^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\ln }^{2}⁡2-{2}^{x}{\\ln }^{2}⁡2 & >0 \\\\ {\\ln }^{2}⁡2(2-{2}^{x}) & >0 \\\\ 2-{2}^{x} & >0 \\\\ 2 & >{2}^{x} \\\\ 1 & >x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186637331 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{12}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }12\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{12}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }12\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637554 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}\\frac{9}{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}\\frac{9}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637554 :question/worked-solution "First, we use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}\\frac{9}{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}9{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}9{csc}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}{csc}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{9}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}9{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}{csc}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-3(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =-3(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1) \\\\  & =3-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}\\frac{9}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}9{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}9{\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}{\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{9}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}9{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}{\\csc }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\csc }^{2}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\csc }^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-3(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =-3(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1) \\\\  & =3-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637565 :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to approximate the value of $csc\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$csc\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to approximate the value of $\\csc \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\csc \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186637565 :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =csc\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-csc\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)cot\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{cot}^{2}⁡x+{csc}^{3}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =csc\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right){cot}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{csc}^{3}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =1+(0)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{(1)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4$ into the above to approximate $csc\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4) & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{2}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ f\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cot \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{\\cot }^{2}⁡x+{\\csc }^{3}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\csc \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right){cot}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{csc}^{3}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =1+(0)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{(1)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4$ into the above to approximate $\\csc \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\csc \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4) & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0.4-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{2}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186637849 :answer/value "-{cot}^{3}⁡t" "-{\\cot }^{3}⁡t"] [:db/cas 17592186637879 :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡(3t)" "{\\cot }^{2}⁡(3t)"] [:db/cas 17592186637906 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cot}^{3}⁡(2t)" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\cot }^{3}⁡(2t)"] [:db/cas 17592186637946 :answer/value "-{cot}^{3}⁡(2t)" "-{\\cot }^{3}⁡(2t)"] [:db/cas 17592186638247 :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{sec}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t)}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2t))}{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-{cot}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{\\sec }^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2{\\sec }^{2}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t)}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2t))}{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-{\\cot }^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186638262 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2}-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}\\,-\\,1)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)=2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\,-\\,1)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=0-2\\cdot 2=-4.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=\\det ({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2}-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}\\,-\\,1)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)=2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\,-\\,1)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=0-2\\cdot 2=-4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186638272 :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x-3 & =-({x}^{2}+4x+3) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+[{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}]+3) \\\\  & =-((x+2)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+2).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-3/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =arcsin\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x-3 & =-({x}^{2}+4x+3) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+[{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}]+3) \\\\  & =-((x+2)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+2).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-3/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\arcsin \\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186638274 :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $C$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(C)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(C)=(-5)\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot 10=-5\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(C)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2/5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C{C}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2/5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {C}^{-1}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2/5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $C$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{\\det (C)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det (C)=(-5)\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot 10=-5\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{\\det (C)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2/5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C{C}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2/5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {C}^{-1}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2/5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639179 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=3$ and $det(A)>0$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=3$ and $\\det (A)>0$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186639179 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=3$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=3$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186639249 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=5$ and $det(A)=1$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=5$ and $\\det (A)=1$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite"] [:db/cas 17592186639249 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=5$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=5>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=5$ and \n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=5>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186639256 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x+1)\\, & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡(x+1)\\, & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639317 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3v & -7 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3v)\\cdot (-3)-(-7)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =9v+56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=-16$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =-16 \\\\ 9v+56 & =-16 \\\\ 9v & =-16-56 \\\\ 9v & =-72 \\\\ v & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3v & -7 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3v)\\cdot (-3)-(-7)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =9v+56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (U)=-16$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (U) & =-16 \\\\ 9v+56 & =-16 \\\\ 9v & =-16-56 \\\\ 9v & =-72 \\\\ v & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639318 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-6-8r & 2-6r \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(U)=182$, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}-6-8r & 2-6r \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $\\det (U)=182$, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186639318 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6-8r & 2-6r \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-6-8r)\\cdot 7-(2-6r)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-42-56r-10+30r \\\\  & =-52-26r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=182,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =182 \\\\ -52-26r & =182 \\\\ -26r & =234 \\\\ r & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6-8r & 2-6r \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-6-8r)\\cdot 7-(2-6r)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-42-56r-10+30r \\\\  & =-52-26r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (U)=182,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (U) & =182 \\\\ -52-26r & =182 \\\\ -26r & =234 \\\\ r & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639325 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-4)-k\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =8+8k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(C)=16$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =16 \\\\ 8+8k & =16 \\\\ 8k & =16-8 \\\\ 8k & =8 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-4)-k\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =8+8k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (C)=16$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (C) & =16 \\\\ 8+8k & =16 \\\\ 8k & =16-8 \\\\ 8k & =8 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639327 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ -4k & 8k+2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (8k+2)-(-6)\\cdot (-4k) \\\\  & =-48k-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=-6$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =-6 \\\\ -48k-6 & =-6 \\\\ -48k & =-6-(-6) \\\\ -48k & =0 \\\\ k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ -4k & 8k+2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (8k+2)-(-6)\\cdot (-4k) \\\\  & =-48k-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (U)=-6$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (U) & =-6 \\\\ -48k-6 & =-6 \\\\ -48k & =-6-(-6) \\\\ -48k & =0 \\\\ k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639331 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ 7k & 7k-5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\cdot (7k-5)-7\\cdot 7k \\\\  & =7k-40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=-89$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =-89 \\\\ 7k-40 & =-89 \\\\ 7k & =-89-(-40) \\\\ 7k & =-49 \\\\ k & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ 7k & 7k-5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\cdot (7k-5)-7\\cdot 7k \\\\  & =7k-40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (U)=-89$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (U) & =-89 \\\\ 7k-40 & =-89 \\\\ 7k & =-89-(-40) \\\\ 7k & =-49 \\\\ k & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639340 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$ given that\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -6 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{7} & 7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n$det(D)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\det (D)$ given that\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & -6 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{7} & 7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n$\\det (D)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186639341 :question/problem "Calculate $det(E)$ given that\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$E=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n$det(E)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate $\\det (E)$ given that\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$E=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n$\\det (E)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186639345 :question/problem "Calculate $det(C)$, if\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}-4x & -4y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 8y\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" "Calculate $\\det (C)$, if\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}-4x & -4y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 8y\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186639354 :question/problem "Calculate $det(H)$, if\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$H=\\begin{bmatrix}-2t & 7u \\\\ -2r & 7s\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" "Calculate $\\det (H)$, if\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$H=\\begin{bmatrix}-2t & 7u \\\\ -2r & 7s\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186639363 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$, if\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -2 \\\\ y & xy\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" "Calculate $\\det (D)$, if\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6x & -2 \\\\ y & xy\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186639371 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5+p & -2p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -4-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=38,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5+p & -2p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -4-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $\\det (A)=38,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186639371 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5+p & -2p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -4-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-5+p)(-4-p)-(-2p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =20+5p-4p-{p}^{2}+2p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =20+3p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =20+3p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $det(A)=38$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =38 \\\\ 20+3p & =38 \\\\ 3p & =18 \\\\ p & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5+p & -2p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -4-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-5+p)(-4-p)-(-2p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =20+5p-4p-{p}^{2}+2p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =20+3p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =20+3p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\det (A)=38$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =38 \\\\ 20+3p & =38 \\\\ 3p & =18 \\\\ p & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639375 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7+p & -6p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=23,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7+p & -6p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $\\det (A)=23,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186639375 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}7+p & -6p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(7+p)(2-p)-(-6p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14-7p+2p-{p}^{2}+6p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =14+p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =14+p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $det(A)=23$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =23 \\\\ 14+p & =23 \\\\ p & =9 \\\\ p & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}7+p & -6p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(7+p)(2-p)-(-6p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14-7p+2p-{p}^{2}+6p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =14+p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =14+p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\det (A)=23$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =23 \\\\ 14+p & =23 \\\\ p & =9 \\\\ p & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639377 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4+p & 7p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -5-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=-180,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4+p & 7p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -5-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $\\det (A)=-180,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186639377 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4+p & 7p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -5-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4+p)(-5-p)-(7p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-20-4p-5p-{p}^{2}-7p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-20-16p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =-20-16p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-180$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =-180 \\\\ -20-16p & =-180 \\\\ -16p & =-160 \\\\ p & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4+p & 7p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -5-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4+p)(-5-p)-(7p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-20-4p-5p-{p}^{2}-7p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-20-16p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =-20-16p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\det (A)=-180$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =-180 \\\\ -20-16p & =-180 \\\\ -16p & =-160 \\\\ p & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639378 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3+p & 8p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=82,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3+p & 8p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $\\det (A)=82,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186639378 :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3+p & 8p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3+p)(4-p)-(8p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12-3p+4p-{p}^{2}-8p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =12-7p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =12-7p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $det(A)=82$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =82 \\\\ 12-7p & =82 \\\\ -7p & =70 \\\\ p & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3+p & 8p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4-p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3+p)(4-p)-(8p-{p}^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12-3p+4p-{p}^{2}-8p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =12-7p-\\cancel{{p}^{2}}+\\cancel{{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =12-7p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\det (A)=82$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =82 \\\\ 12-7p & =82 \\\\ -7p & =70 \\\\ p & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639970 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant–tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant–tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1={\\sec }^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1 & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639984 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186639984 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x){sec}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x){sec}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x){\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x){\\sec }^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186640027 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Squaring the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Squaring the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1={\\sec }^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1 & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186640046 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186640046 :question/worked-solution "We use the identities $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-2) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identities $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-2) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641627 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186641627 :question/worked-solution "From the identity $1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ with $\\theta =2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n1-{sin}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})={cos}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the identity $1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta$ with $\\theta =2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})={\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186641641 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{2}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{cot}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{\\cot }^{2}⁡x-{\\csc }^{2}⁡x}{{\\csc }^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186641641 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{2}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{cot}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}{(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)+{x}^{2}-{cot}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{1+{cot}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{cot}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{(-1)}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,{|}_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x={\\csc }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{\\cot }^{2}⁡x-{\\csc }^{2}⁡x}{{\\csc }^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{\\cot }^{2}⁡x-(1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)}{(1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x)+{x}^{2}-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{{\\cot }^{2}⁡x-1-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}{1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}-{\\cot }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{(-1)}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,{|}_{1/\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642317 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186642351 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{64}=1$ in $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨y,z⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{64} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{64} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,x) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{64}=1$ in $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨y,z⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{64} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{64} \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,x) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"] [:db/cas 17592186643139 :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & ={b}_{11}{C}_{11}+{b}_{12}{C}_{12}+{b}_{13}{C}_{13}+{b}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+5\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =5{C}_{13} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 5 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & 0 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & ∗ & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\left(2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =5(2\\cdot (-4\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 3)-0+0) \\\\  & =5(-24) \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & ={b}_{11}{C}_{11}+{b}_{12}{C}_{12}+{b}_{13}{C}_{13}+{b}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+5\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =5{C}_{13} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 5 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & 0 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & ∗ & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\left(2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =5(2\\cdot (-4\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 3)-0+0) \\\\  & =5(-24) \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186645298 :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡(xy)-y=c" "{\\ln }^{2}⁡(xy)-y=c"] [:db/cas 17592186645726 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=12u+3v-5,\\,y=4u+v.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $det(A)=0$\n\nIII. The transformation is invertible" "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=12u+3v-5,\\,y=4u+v.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $\\det (A)=0$\n\nIII. The transformation is invertible"] [:db/cas 17592186645726 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}12u+3v-5 \\\\ 4u+v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12u+3v \\\\ 4u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 12-3\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)=0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}12u+3v-5 \\\\ 4u+v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12u+3v \\\\ 4u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}12 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 12-3\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\det (A)=0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186645730 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u+5v+9,\\,y=5-6u-5v.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $det(A)=10$\n\nIII. The transformation is invertible" "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u+5v+9,\\,y=5-6u-5v.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $\\det (A)=10$\n\nIII. The transformation is invertible"] [:db/cas 17592186645730 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+5v+9 \\\\ 5-6u-5v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+5v \\\\ -6u-5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{vmatrix}=(-5)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot (-6)=10\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+5v+9 \\\\ 5-6u-5v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+5v \\\\ -6u-5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{vmatrix}=(-5)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot (-6)=10\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\det (A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186646266 :question/problem "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)={x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡(4)-{4}^{x}$ is concave up.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)={x}^{2}{\\ln }^{2}⁡(4)-{4}^{x}$ is concave up.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$"] [:db/cas 17592186646266 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)={x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡(4)-{4}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x{ln}^{2}⁡(4)-{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{ln}^{2}⁡(4)-{4}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{ln}^{2}⁡(4)-{4}^{x}{ln}^{2}⁡(4) & >0 \\\\ {ln}^{2}⁡(4)\\,(2-{4}^{x}) & >0 \\\\ 2-{4}^{x} & >0 \\\\ {4}^{x} & <2 \\\\ ({2}^{2})^{x} & <2 \\\\ {2}^{2x} & <{2}^{1} \\\\ 2x & <1 \\\\ x & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2}).$" "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)={x}^{2}{\\ln }^{2}⁡(4)-{4}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x{\\ln }^{2}⁡(4)-{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{\\ln }^{2}⁡(4)-{4}^{x}{\\ln }^{2}⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\ln }^{2}⁡(4)-{4}^{x}{\\ln }^{2}⁡(4) & >0 \\\\ {\\ln }^{2}⁡(4)\\,(2-{4}^{x}) & >0 \\\\ 2-{4}^{x} & >0 \\\\ {4}^{x} & <2 \\\\ ({2}^{2})^{x} & <2 \\\\ {2}^{2x} & <{2}^{1} \\\\ 2x & <1 \\\\ x & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186646312 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-1 \\\\ 2u-v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ 2u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 0=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-1|=1.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-1 \\\\ 2u-v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ 2u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 0=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|\\det (A)|=|-1|=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647767 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 7 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -6 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 7 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -6 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=(-9)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot 2=54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|54|=54.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 7 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -6 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|\\det (M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\det (M)=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 7 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -6 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=(-9)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot 2=54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|\\det (M)|=|54|=54.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647783 :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+35 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+({6}^{2}-{6}^{2})+35 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2})-36+35 \\\\  & =(x-6)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{6+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{8}\\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x=10{\\int }_{6+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{8}\\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-6).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $6+2/\\sqrt{3}$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2/\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{8}\\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10{\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{arcsec}\\,u\\,{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =10(\\text{arcsec}(2)-\\text{arcsec}\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+35 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+({6}^{2}-{6}^{2})+35 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2})-36+35 \\\\  & =(x-6)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{6+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{8}\\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x=10{\\int }_{6+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{8}\\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-6).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $6+2/\\sqrt{3}$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2/\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{8}\\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10{\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =10\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u\\,{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =10(\\operatorname{arcsec} (2)-\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647806 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $1$st row contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(L) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =4\\cdot (3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-3))=72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(L)=72.\n$$" "Notice that the $1$st row contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(L) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =4\\cdot (3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-3))=72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det (L)=72.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647822 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)=12 \\\\ det(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det({A}^{T}) & =det(A)=12 \\\\ det({B}^{T}) & =det(B)=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{T})+det({B}^{T})=12+12=24.\n$$" "The determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)=12 \\\\ \\det (B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det ({A}^{T}) & =\\det (A)=12 \\\\ \\det ({B}^{T}) & =\\det (B)=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det ({A}^{T})+\\det ({B}^{T})=12+12=24.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186648639 :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(A)=28.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=-\\frac{3}{7}{R}_{1}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" "Suppose $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $\\det (A)=28.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=-\\frac{3}{7}{R}_{1}.$ What is the value of $\\det (B)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186648639 :question/worked-solution "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $-\\frac{3}{7}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-\\frac{3}{7}det(A) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $-\\frac{3}{7}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =-\\frac{3}{7}\\det (A) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186649198 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3v-4u+2 \\\\ 8u-6v+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4u+3v \\\\ 8u-6v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -6\\end{vmatrix}=(-6)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 8=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{1}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6v-2u-7 \\\\ 6-3u-v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2u+6v \\\\ -3u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot (-2)-6\\cdot (-3)=20\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{2}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-3u+3v \\\\ 7u-8v-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3u+3v \\\\ 7u-8v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -8\\end{vmatrix}=(-8)\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot 7=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{3}$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{2}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only.\"" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $\\det (A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3v-4u+2 \\\\ 8u-6v+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4u+3v \\\\ 8u-6v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -6\\end{vmatrix}=(-6)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 8=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{1}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6v-2u-7 \\\\ 6-3u-v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2u+6v \\\\ -3u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot (-2)-6\\cdot (-3)=20\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{2}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-3u+3v \\\\ 7u-8v-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3u+3v \\\\ 7u-8v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -8\\end{vmatrix}=(-8)\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot 7=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{3}$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{2}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186649887 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=0 \\\\ 2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich  has the only solution $x=0$ and $y=0.$  Hence, $(0,0)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x)=4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(34x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=4\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=8>0,\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=4>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$ \n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=0 \\\\ 2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich  has the only solution $x=0$ and $y=0.$  Hence, $(0,0)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x)=4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(34x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=\\det ({f}^{″}(0,0))=4\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=8>0,\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=4>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$ \n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186650932 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta , \\\\ y & =(2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-4{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(-4)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-4,b=-2,$ and so \n\n$$\na+b=(-4)+(-2)=-6.\n$$" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta , \\\\ y & =(2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-4{({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(-4)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-4,b=-2,$ and so \n\n$$\na+b=(-4)+(-2)=-6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186650933 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}-2({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )-2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +(-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-2,b=-1,$ and so \n\n$$\na+b=-2+(-1)=-3.\n$$" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}-2({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )-2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +(-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-2,b=-1,$ and so \n\n$$\na+b=-2+(-1)=-3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186650942 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} &=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2.\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{0}{-2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(0)}{-2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} &=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2.\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-2{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{0}{-2{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta }=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(0)}{-2{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-2{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186650995 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} &=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=7,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{(-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{(-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} &=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=7,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{(-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta }{(-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}{\\csc }^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186651555 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(k) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(k),{1}_{B}(k)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(k)=0,$ we conclude that $k$ does not belong to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\min \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(k) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(k),{1}_{B}(k)\\} \\\\  & =\\max \\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(k)=0,$ we conclude that $k$ does not belong to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186651583 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-4) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(-4),{1}_{B}(-4)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-4)=1,$ we conclude that $-4$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\min \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-4) & =\\max \\{{1}_{A}(-4),{1}_{B}(-4)\\} \\\\  & =\\max \\{0,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-4)=1,$ we conclude that $-4$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186654246 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $M-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nM-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{4}-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(M-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{4}-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3}-\\lambda )(\\frac{7}{4}-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -\\frac{1}{3})(\\lambda -\\frac{7}{4}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." "Let's start by writing the matrix $M-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nM-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{4}-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (M-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{4}-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3}-\\lambda )(\\frac{7}{4}-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -\\frac{1}{3})(\\lambda -\\frac{7}{4}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal."] [:db/cas 17592186654292 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing the matrix $M-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nM-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(M-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-11\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ -7-7\\lambda +\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+22 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-6)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =36-60 \\\\  & =-24 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $M$ does not have real eigenvalues." "Let's start by writing the matrix $M-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nM-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (M-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (M-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-11\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ -7-7\\lambda +\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+22 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-6)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =36-60 \\\\  & =-24 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $M$ does not have real eigenvalues."] [:db/cas 17592186654798 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|22-(-14)|,|-13-21|) \\\\  & =max(36,34) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=\\max (|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\max (|22-(-14)|,|-13-21|) \\\\  & =\\max (36,34) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186654800 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|-5-9|,|2-(-8)|) \\\\  & =max(14,10) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=\\max (|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\max (|-5-9|,|2-(-8)|) \\\\  & =\\max (14,10) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186654953 :answer/value "\\frac{25}{8}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /6}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta" "\\frac{25}{8}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /6}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta"] [:db/cas 17592186655081 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{7\\pi /6}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{7\\pi /6}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta"] [:db/cas 17592186655119 :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta" "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta"] [:db/cas 17592186655133 :answer/value "\\frac{25}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(3+\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta" "\\frac{25}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3+\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta"] [:db/cas 17592186655481 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu$ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=5{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =20-15-6 \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =45-3+16 \\\\  & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-1,58).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\ge 6).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 6) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{6-(-1)}{\\sqrt{58}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.92)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.92) & =1-P(Z<0.92) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.92) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.92).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.92)=0.8212.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.92) &=1-0.8212=0.1788.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 6)=0.1788.\n$$" "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu$ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ \\operatorname{Var} [Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=5{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =20-15-6 \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =45-3+16 \\\\  & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-1,58).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\ge 6).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 6) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{6-(-1)}{\\sqrt{58}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.92)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.92) & =1-P(Z<0.92) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.92) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.92).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.92)=0.8212.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.92) &=1-0.8212=0.1788.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 6)=0.1788.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655485 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu$ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}+3{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2-2-3 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =9-5+60 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-3,64).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y>0).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>0) & =P(Z>\\frac{0-(-3)}{\\sqrt{64}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>0.38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>0.38) & =1-P(Z\\le 0.38) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.38).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.38)=0.6480.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>0.38) &=1-0.6480=0.3520.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y>0)=0.3520.\n$$" "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu$ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ \\operatorname{Var} [Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}+3{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2-2-3 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =9-5+60 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-3,64).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y>0).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>0) & =P(Z>\\frac{0-(-3)}{\\sqrt{64}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>0.38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>0.38) & =1-P(Z\\le 0.38) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.38).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.38)=0.6480.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>0.38) &=1-0.6480=0.3520.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y>0)=0.3520.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655487 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )}{2{\\theta }^{2}}$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /6}{\\left(\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )}{2{\\theta }^{2}}\\right)}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{2}}{2\\cdot {2}^{2}}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /6}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{8}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /6}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )}{2{\\theta }^{2}}$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /6}{\\left(\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )}{2{\\theta }^{2}}\\right)}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{2}}{2\\cdot {2}^{2}}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /6}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{8}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /6}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}{{\\theta }^{4}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186655488 :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})}$\n\nII. $\\text{mode}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})$\n\nIII. $min({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})-max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})$" "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})}$\n\nII. $\\text{mode}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})$\n\nIII. $\\min ({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})-\\max ({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})$"] [:db/cas 17592186655493 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector 